== A Master’s Own Master in Heaven

=== §. 46. #emph[Of the subjection under which masters are.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 6:9.]

    #text(style: "italic")[Knowing that your#super[+] Master also is in heaven, and there is no respect of persons with Him.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

In general the apostle gives only one reason to stir masters up to do their duties, but it is so laid down that it includes other forceful reasons under it.

The principal reason is taken from the subjection masters are under.

The other reasons are taken from the description of the authority under which masters are; for it is such an authority that:

1\. In relation to it there is no difference between master and servant.

2\. It far surpasses all dignities on earth.

3\. It is swayed by no outward respect for anything.

The first reason, which declares the subjection of masters in that they have a master over them, reminds them of the account they are to make, and the reckoning they are to give, of the right use of their authority, and of their conduct toward those who are under them. For they are only like stewards over fellow servants; every one of them therefore will hear this charge: “Give an account of your stewardship.” #footnote[Luke 16:2.]

In this respect this reason is both #emph[a spur] and #emph[a curb] to masters.

It is #emph[a spur,] to prick them forward to perform conscientiously all the duties which are required of them; for they have a master who will take notice of it and reward them for it. As they approve and reward the good service which their servants do, so much more will their master approve and reward them if they do well. Do masters therefore expect their servants to perform their duty? Then let them perform theirs, for the same reason holds for both. Let this be applied to all the particular duties mentioned before.

It is also #emph[a curb,] to restrain masters from doing anything to their servants but what they will be able to justify to their own master. With this curb God held in the Israelites, saying, “You are not to rule over your servant harshly, but you shall fear your God.” Joseph#footnote[Lev 25:43.] was held in by it when he said, “I fear God. So do this and you#super[+] will live.”#footnote[Gen 42:18.] And Nehemiah, when he said, “The governors before me had heavily burdened the people … but I did not do this because of my fear of God.”#footnote[Neh 5:15.] And Job, when he said, “If I have rejected the cause of my manservant … what will I do when God rises to judge? How will I answer when called to account?”#footnote[Job 31:13, 14.] Think of this, O masters, when you#super[+] are about to demand anything of your#super[+] servants that is not lawful or fitting; when you#super[+] are incensed and stirred up in anger to strike your#super[+] servants unjustly or cruelly; when you#super[+] withhold from them anything that is their due; when you#super[+] lay more on them than they are able to bear; when in any way you#super[+] wrong or oppress them. Think, and say to yourselves#super[+], #emph[Can this be justified? How will we be able to hold up our heads before our master when he calls us to account?] What stronger motive to do every duty? What stronger restraint from all injustice and severity? The notion many have that they are free, under no one, and to give no account, makes them both negligent of their own duty and insolent toward others; as Pharaoh, who said, “Who is the #smallcaps[Lord] that I should obey His voice?”#footnote[Exod 5:2.] and Sennacherib, who said, “Will your#super[+] God deliver you#super[+] from my hand?”#footnote[2 Chr 32:15.] and Nebuchadnezzar, who said, “What god will be able to deliver you#super[+] from my hands?”#footnote[Dan 3:15.] Note the outcome of this insolence. Pharaoh, after many extraordinary plagues laid on him and his people, #footnote[Exod 14:28.] was drowned in the Red Sea with all his army. Sennacherib,#footnote[Isa 37:36, 38.] after his army was destroyed, was killed by his own sons. Nebuchadnezzar#footnote[Dan 4:30.] became a very beast. But to let these abominable blasphemers pass, there are many masters who, though they do not utter such detestable blasphemies with their mouths, yet by their conduct toward their servants show themselves to be little better minded, in that they make their own will a rule for their servants, and will have them do things that are unfitting and unlawful, using their servants as slaves, or rather as beasts. Let all such masters know that they have a master.

=== §. 47. #emph[Of the equality between masters and servants in relation to God.]

The second reason, in this particle #emph[also (your#super[+] Master also),] declares an equality between masters and servants in relation to God. As God is the master of servants, so he is the master of masters #emph[also.] As servants are the Lord’s #emph[freemen,]#footnote[1 Cor 7:22.] so masters are the Lord’s #emph[servants.] In this respect those who are made rulers, and those who are under them, are called #emph[fellow servants.]#footnote[Matt 24:49. #emph[Dominus fidelem habens seruum diligat vt fratrem propter fidei societatem salua sibi seruitute. Constit. Apost. l.] 4. #emph[c.] 12. #emph[Dominum patrem familiae appellauerunt, seruos familiares. Senec. Epist.] 47. \[They called the master the father of the household, and the servants members of the household.\]] For however great the difference between master and servant in outward dignity, yet as the servants of God they are in a like condition, and in many things may be counted equal; especially if both are of the same faith, and so brethren in Christ. This is another #emph[spur,] and a #emph[curb] too.

A #emph[spur,] in that God will accept all the more kindly the goodness which masters do to their servants, because it is done to God’s servants.

A #emph[curb,] in that servants will be heard before God as well as masters, for he is the master of both.

Many think that all the kindness shown to servants is lost, because servants are so lowly that they are able to make no repayment. But their master is able.

Others think their servants can never take any revenge on them, and so use them as they please. But the master of servants, who is also the master of masters, can take vengeance, even such as will make masters sorely repent all the wrong they have done.

If masters duly weighed this point, that, though there is some difference between them and their servants in outward government, yet before God they are like fellow servants, would they be over-harsh and cruel? Would they not be kind and gentle?

=== §. 48. #emph[Of God’s being in heaven, how it is a motive to stir up masters to have due regard for their servants.]

The third reason (taken from the place of God, #emph[in heaven]) declares the surpassing excellence of that great master who is the common master of masters and of servants, and it gives an edge to the earlier motives.

1\. It shows that though the wrong which masters do to their servants is within the walls of their house, so that no mortal eye can see it, yet God who is in heaven sees it; and though servants cannot be admitted into the courts of men to make their complaint, yet heaven is open to them: they may have access to that great master who is in heaven whenever they will. What made the wicked steward#footnote[Matt 24:48.] deal harshly with his fellows was the notion he had of his master’s absence. But those who know that this great master is in heaven can have no such notion of him; for they know #footnote[Prov 15:3.] that as heaven is over every place, so “the eyes of the #smallcaps[Lord] are in every place, observing the evil and the good”: he sees all the good and all the evil that masters do to their servants.

2\. It shows that the kindness which the Lord will repay, and the vengeance which he will inflict, are infinitely greater than the good or evil that masters can do to their servants, even as heaven is higher than earth, and as he who is in heaven is greater than those who are on earth. Do you, therefore, who are a master on earth, gladden or grieve the soul of your servant? God in heaven can much more make your soul glad or sad. Do you therefore desire the favor, or fear the frowns, of your master in heaven? Show favor to your servant on earth, and forbear threatening. Remember the infinite disparity between your mastership and God’s, and this will move you all the more to deal with your servant as you would have God deal with you.

3\. It shows that there is much more reason for us to take notice of our servants, of their pains, of their diligence and of their faithfulness, than for God to take notice of ours; and less reason for us to scorn or neglect our servants than for God to scorn or neglect us. For there is a far greater difference between God and us than between us and our servants. We and our #footnote[#emph[Vis tu cogitare istum quem seruum tuum vocas, exijsdem seminibus ortum, eodem frui coelo, aequè spirare, aequè viuere, aequè mori. Senec. Epist.] 47.] servants are all of the earth, of the same mold, nature and disposition, subject to the same passions and to the same dissolution. (The heathen observed as much.) But God is in heaven, eternal, unchangeable, in every way surpassingly glorious. How then can we expect to be regarded by this master if we do not regard our servants? O masters, in all your#super[+] dealings with your#super[+] servants, remember #emph[your#super[+] Master is in heaven.]

=== §. 49. #emph[Of God’s impartial respect toward all.]

The fourth reason (in these words, #emph[and there is no respect of persons with Him]) declares God’s just and equal way of proceeding with all men, of whatever rank and degree. God will do the same things to all sorts of masters that they do to their servants. The apostle calls masters to consider this impartial justice of God, both because of the outward power which they have over their servants, and also because for the most part masters are backed by the power and authority of magistrates on earth, who in matters of dispute between master and servant are ordinarily partial, having more respect for masters than for servants.

But let masters here learn to cast off all such foolish notions and vain hopes. Though they are higher in place, and have more wealth and better connections than their servants, and though men who have carnal eyes may be much moved by this to respect them, yet God will not go a hair’s breadth from justice for the whole world. If the greatest man that ever was in the world had a servant who was the lowliest that ever was, and a case between that master and that servant came before God, God would not lean in the least toward that master more than toward the servant. If the greatest abuse the lowliest, they will not escape. Therefore, O masters, give no just cause of complaint to any servant.

The apostle’s way of setting down these points, marked by this word #emph[(knowing),] implies that ignorance of God, of the authority which he has over masters, of the equality between masters and servants in relation to God, and of God’s heavenly excellence and impartial respect toward all, makes masters abuse their power, by neglecting all duty, and by lording it over their servants and tyrannizing over them. Therefore you#super[+] who have been ignorant of these points until now, learn them now; and you#super[+] who know them, call them often to mind, and do what becomes good masters, “knowing that your#super[+] Master also is in heaven, and there is no respect of persons with Him.”
